## Diagrams

Diagrams are plugin-illustrator renders. The `diagram` block is the source; regenerate the image with
`moon run plugin-illustrator:render-diagrams -- <abs path>/diagrams/<name>.dx`.

### Connect and catch up

![On open the client claims the database lock, opens SQLite in the OPFS worker and the socket; it sends pull with its saved seq, merges the records the store answers with into the database through the ECHO API, then reads its local records, pushes the ones that differ from base, and on the ack moves base to the stamped versions.](diagrams/connect.dx.svg)

```diagram
diagram kind=sequence
node UI "App" ref="packages/apps/echo-sqlite-demo/src/client/components/App.tsx"
node Sync "connect()" ref="packages/core/echo/echo-sqlite-sync/src/sync.ts"
node DB "SqliteDatabase" ref="packages/core/echo/echo-sqlite/src/database.ts"
node DO "SpaceObject" ref="packages/core/echo/echo-sync-worker/src/space-object.ts"
edge UI -> Sync "connect(db, slug)"
edge Sync sends DO "pull since=seq"
edge DO sends Sync "records from..seq"
edge Sync calls DB "merge: add, update"
edge DB returns Sync "flushed"
edge Sync calls DB "read local records"
edge DB returns Sync "changed vs base"
edge Sync sends DO "push clientSeq+1"
edge DO sends Sync "ack accepted"
note Sync "base = stamped"
edge Sync -> UI "onSync"
```

### Two clients edit one record

![Client A edits the name of record x and client B edits its email, both on top of x at seq 4. B's push lands first as x at seq 5 and is broadcast to A. A merges three-way: name from A, email from B, base moves to seq 5. A's earlier push, based on 4, is rejected; A pushes the merged x based on 5, which is stamped 6 and broadcast to B, so both hold name and email.](diagrams/concurrent-edit.dx.svg)

```diagram
diagram kind=sequence
node A "client A" ref="packages/core/echo/echo-sqlite-sync/src/sync.ts"
node DO "SpaceObject" ref="packages/core/echo/echo-sync-worker/src/space-object.ts"
node B "client B" ref="packages/core/echo/echo-sqlite-sync/src/sync.ts"
note A "edit x.name"
note B "edit x.email"
edge B sends DO "push x base=4"
edge A sends DO "push x base=4"
edge DO sends B "ack, x@5"
edge DO sends A "records x@5"
note A "merge3: name A, email B"
edge DO sends A "ack rejected [x]"
edge A sends DO "push x base=5"
edge DO sends A "ack, x@6"
edge DO sends B "records x@6"
```

### Data the client keeps

![The client keeps SyncState in localStorage per slug: its client id, clientSeq, the seq it has applied, the store's incarnation, and base, the last agreed SyncRecord per key. Local records are read from the SqliteDatabase; a record whose fingerprint differs from its base is pushed with base's seq.](diagrams/state.dx.svg)

```diagram
diagram flow=right
node State "SyncState" ref="packages/core/echo/echo-sqlite-sync/src/sync.ts"
node Base "base[key]" ref="packages/core/echo/echo-sqlite-sync/src/sync.ts"
node Local "local record" ref="packages/core/echo/echo-sqlite-sync/src/sync.ts"
node DB "SqliteDatabase" ref="packages/core/echo/echo-sqlite/src/database.ts"
node Push "PushRecord" ref="packages/core/echo/echo-sync-worker/src/protocol.ts"
edge State one-to-many Base
edge DB -> Local "readLocal"
edge Local -> Push "differs from base"
edge Base -> Push "base = seq"
```

## Types

```mdl
type SyncState
  desc: |
    What one browser remembers about one graph, in localStorage under `echo-sqlite-demo:<slug>`. Lost
    or unreadable state means a full re-sync, never lost edits: the edits are in the database.
  fields:
    client: string                       # random; names this browser to the store
    clientSeq: number                    # the number of this client's last push
    seq: number                          # the highest store seq applied here
    incarnation?: string                 # the store the seq belongs to
    base: Record<string, SyncRecord>     # per key, the last version the store and this client agreed on
```

`SyncRecord` and `PushRecord` are `org.dxos.echo-sync-worker` types.

## Features

```mdl
feat F-1: The sync loop

  req F-1.1:
    when: the socket opens
    then: the client sends `pull` with its saved seq, then pushes whatever differs from `base`

  req F-1.2:
    when: a record differs from its `base` (by a fingerprint of typename, props, refs and deleted, with
          object keys sorted)
    then: it is pushed with `base` set to its base's seq, or 0 when it has none

  req F-1.3:
    when: a push is outstanding
    then: no other push is sent; the next one goes when its answer arrives

  req F-1.4:
    when: a `records` message arrives with `from` above the client's seq
    then: its records are merged but the seq does not move, and the client pulls from its seq

  req F-1.5:
    when: a push is rejected more than 5 times in a row
    then: the client stops retrying and reports an error until the next local change
```

```mdl
feat F-2: Merge

  req F-2.1:
    when: a remote record arrives for a key with no local change since `base`
    then: the remote record is applied as is

  req F-2.2:
    when: a remote record arrives for a key changed locally since `base`
    then: each field takes the local value if it differs from base and the remote value otherwise, for
          props and refs alike, and `base` moves to the remote record

  req F-2.3:
    when: either side deleted the record
    then: the merge is a tombstone

  req F-2.4:
    when: a remote record is this client's own write coming back with its seq
    then: only `base` moves; the local record, with any edits made since, stays as it is

  req F-2.5:
    when: a local record lacks an edge its base has
    then: the edge keeps the base's value, because an edge whose target is not loaded is omitted when
          read, and only `null` means cleared
```

```mdl
feat F-3: Applying remote records

  req F-3.1:
    when: merged records are written
    then: scalars and tombstones go first through `db.add`, `Obj.update` and `db.remove`, then edges, so
          every target exists before an edge to it is set
```

```mdl
feat F-4: Store reset

  req F-4.1:
    when: a message names an incarnation other than the saved one
    then: the client forgets `base` and its seq, pulls from 0, and pushes its whole local graph
```

```mdl
feat F-5: One tab per browser

  req F-5.1:
    when: a second tab of the same origin opens the app
    then: it does not open the database and says that the app is open in another tab, because OPFS
          gives one file handle per file
  req F-5.2:
    when: the tab holding the database closes
    then: a waiting tab opens the app without a manual reload
```

## Acceptance

```mdl
scenario T-1: Edits to different fields both survive
  given: base {name, email}; local changed name; remote changed email
  when: they are merged
  then: the result has the local name and the remote email
  verifies: [F-2.2]
```

```mdl
scenario T-2: The same field keeps the local edit
  given: base {name}; both sides changed name
  when: they are merged
  then: the result has the local name
  verifies: [F-2.2]
```

```mdl
scenario T-3: A delete wins
  given: one side deleted the record and the other edited it
  when: they are merged
  then: the result is a tombstone
  verifies: [F-2.3]
```

```mdl
scenario T-4: A missing edge keeps the base value
  given: base refs {employer: x, manager: m}; local refs {manager: null}
  when: the local record is put on its base
  then: refs are {employer: x, manager: null}
  verifies: [F-2.5]
```

```mdl
scenario T-5: A different incarnation is a reset
  given: saved incarnation a
  when: a message names incarnation b
  then: it is a reset; the same incarnation or a first sync is not
  verifies: [F-4.1]
```

## Changelog

```mdl
decision D-1: Sync orders writes with a sequence number the server keeps and a counter each client keeps, never with timestamps. A round is: the client pulls, merges, and pushes.
  date: 2026-11-10
  by: user:dmaretskyi
  affects: [F-1, F-2]
  source: https://claude.ai/code/session_01LepGAYKGht2b6Gyq1s1W6W
```

```mdl
decision D-2: The browser keeps one database per origin, held by one tab, and the app shows the user why a second tab cannot open it.
  date: 2026-11-10
  by: user:dmaretskyi
  affects: [F-5]
  source: https://claude.ai/code/session_01LepGAYKGht2b6Gyq1s1W6W
```

```mdl
decision D-3: The merge is three-way and field by field against `base`, and a delete on either side wins. Fills in [D-1].
  date: 2026-11-10
  by: agent:claude-code
  for: user:dmaretskyi
  affects: [F-2]
  why: |
    A record-level merge loses a concurrent edit to another field of the same object. A delete wins
    because undoing it would bring back an object whose edges the deleter has already dropped.
  rejected: Last writer wins per record; a CRDT per field, which this toy does not need.
```

```mdl
decision D-4: A waiting tab opens by itself when the tab holding the database closes. Fills in [D-2].
  date: 2026-11-10
  by: agent:claude-code
  for: user:dmaretskyi
  affects: [F-5.2]
  why: The lock is released when the holding tab closes, so the waiting tab can learn that without the user reloading it.
```

```mdl
decision D-5: The database engine stays wa-sqlite; Turso's browser build is not used. Fills in [D-2].
  date: 2026-11-10
  by: agent:claude-code
  for: user:dmaretskyi
  affects: [F-5]
  why: |
    `@tursodatabase/database-wasm` 0.8.3 and 0.9.0-pre.2 open each OPFS file with an exclusive sync access
    handle, the same one-tab limit, and need cross-origin isolation for their thread pool.
  rejected: One database file per tab, each a separate sync peer.
```
